opic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7a4641acb939d6ed43fde7717c3907c03e4ca6ae"},{"id":"q-22c9e4883f61","stem":"List 3 species of parasites that you acquire by ingestion of undercooked meat. (3 marks)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"T.saginata"},{"label":"B","text":"T.solium"},{"label":"C","text":"T. spillaris"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b777fbca74f6","stem":"Name the parasites which cause hydatid disease: (5 marks)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. grnulosus"},{"label":"B","text":"E. multiculosis How is hydatid disease acquired- through ingestion of embryonated eggs of Echinococcus spp Mention the two methods for treatment of hydatid disease"},{"label":"A","text":"______________________________________________________________________"},{"label":"B","text":"_____________________________________________________________________"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-21d744cc4d37","stem":"What is the common name of Trichuris trichiura ( 6 marks) How is Trichuris trichiura Infection acquired? How is Trichuris trichiura infection diagnosed How can Trichuris trichura infection be prevented?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-df79cb468362","stem":"List 4 diseases that can be transmitted via fecal-oral route ( 4 marks)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1141fc6b6fd9","stem":"Schistosoma mansoni primarily differs from Schistosoma haematobium in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Helminthology","topicOriginal":"Helminthology","subtopic":"Schistosomiasis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"geographical distribution"},{"label":"B","text":"definitive host"},{"label":"C","text":"location of adult worms in host tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"mode of transmittance to definitive host e. None of the above"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Helminthology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e3c73416cbd25f966bb75a908e3e76930618f72e"},{"id":"q-9ccf87f06056","stem":"Current medications are trimethoprim/sulfa and indinavir. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) reveals 4 WBC, and budding encapsulated yeast forms grow on Sabouraud's agar. Which of the following is an accurate description of the morphology of the infectious form of the organism responsible for the man's illness?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad-based, budding yeasts"},{"label":"B","text":"Budding yeasts in a \"pilot's wheel\" arrangement"},{"label":"C","text":"Cylindrical arthroconidia"},{"label":"D","text":"Encapsulated budding yeasts"},{"label":"E","text":"Filamentous molds"},{"label":"F","text":"Septate hyphae with microconidia and macroconidia Explanation: The correct answer is D. This is a classic case of Cryptococcus neoformans meningitis. Clues included the patient population (HIV positive), geographic area (Mississippi and Missouri river beds), and diagnostic form (encapsulated yeast). Cryptococcus is a monomorphic fungus, unlike many of the classic pathogens within the fungal group, so the encapsulated yeast form would be found both in clinical specimens and in the environment as the infectious form. Broad-based, budding yeasts (choice A) would be the forms expected to be found in clinical specimens (not environmental forms) from patients infected with Blastomyces dermatitidis, which is far more likely to present with skin and bone lesions than with meningitis."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ba149f7a3033","stem":"Which of the following is an attribute of the causative organism that allows it to produce recurrent infections?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d893d05fd6b4","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d82bed9ede77","stem":"where would the causative agent likely have been found?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-781d66c07b62","stem":"Which of the following organisms most likely caused this pattern of damage?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e08e782460b","stem":"Which of the following organisms would most likely be isolated from the vagina of a normal 5 year-old girl?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66dbb6dabc86","stem":"Which of the following is specifically associated with infection with Schistosoma haematobium?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-036e451889fc","stem":"Which predisposing condition is most likely to be present in this patient?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8724f2907e77","stem":"Which part of the brain is most often affected by septic emboli in patients with infective endocarditis?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c81654d85c5","stem":"Which of the following would be the most likely microscopic finding in a scraping from the rash?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8df07cb60cef","stem":"year-old-man develops fever, chills, and a rash that spreads from his abdomen to cover his extremities. He is seen at the hospital emergency department, where blood is drawn for analysis. Eight days later, the public health department reports the presence of antibody to one of the rickettsial group antigens. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endemic typhus"},{"label":"B","text":"Epidemic typhus"},{"label":"C","text":"Q fever"},{"label":"D","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever"},{"label":"E","text":"Scrub typhus Explanation: The correct answer is A. Endemic typhus is caused by Rickettsia typhi and is found worldwide. It is spread by the feces of the rat flea, and its reservoir is the rat. Epidemic typhus (choice B) is cause by Rickettsia prowazekii. It is prominent during times of war and social upheaval and is spread by the feces of the body louse, Pediculus humanis. Humans are the chief reservoir, although it is also found in populations of flying squirrels along the Atlantic coast of the U.S. Q fever (choice C) is caused by Coxiella burnetii of the family Rickettsiaceae. Unlike the other rickettsial illnesses, Coxiella burnetii is not usually transmitted to humans by the bite of an arthropod. Since the organism is resistant to dehydration, it can be acquired by inhaling dust contaminated by animals with asymptomatic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5a38a8fac4b","stem":"Which of the following antibiotics is most likely responsible for this patient's symptoms?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b2ccc04947b","stem":"Which of the following is a feature of gram-positive bacteria rather than gram-negative bacteria?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5cc078541bf","stem":"Which of the following microorganisms is an obligate aerobe?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5b29b82d353f","stem":"What underlying infection should be considered?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e55e62cdce22","stem":"Which of the following organisms is a natural transformer?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-914b6eeee305","stem":"Which of the following structures is found only in Gram-negative microorganisms?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-389d9b38db53","stem":"which phase of bacterial growth in a closed system?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ec67b6530993","stem":"Which of the following is associated with Babesiosis rather than with Ehrlichiosis or Lyme disease?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-29c366cf7ccb","stem":"Which of the following viruses is capable of replication in enucleated cells?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c810c392948f","stem":"usual colonizing site?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ba9ed00430d3","stem":"An Rh-negative woman married to a heterozygous Rh-positive man has three children. The probability that all three of their children are Rh-positive is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:2."},{"label":"B","text":"1:4."},{"label":"C","text":"1:8."},{"label":"D","text":"zero."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d9315259bbf","stem":"Each of the following statements concerning haptens is correct EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A hapten can combine with (bind to) an antibody."},{"label":"B","text":"A hapten cannot induce an antibody by itself; rather, it must be bound to a carrier protein to be able to induce antibody."},{"label":"C","text":"In both penicillin-induced anaphylaxis and poison ivy, the allergens are haptens."},{"label":"D","text":"Haptens must be processed by CD8+ cells to become immunogenic. Answers (Questions 387-474):"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5829ee3534fe5a18c37c39f4c1e8fec0bc8c0e89"},{"id":"q-62d274b8669b","stem":"D Directions (Questions 475-535): Select the ONE lettered option that is MOST closely associated with the numbered items. Each lettered option may selected once, more than one, nor not at all. Questions 475-480","type":"Multiple Response","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"T cells"},{"label":"B","text":"B cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Macrophages"},{"label":"D","text":"B cells and macrophages"},{"label":"E","text":"T cells, B cells, and macrophages"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4a6d9e7cbfb","stem":"All of the following are true of antigen EXCEPT which one of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They contain epitopes."},{"label":"B","text":"They will react with antibodies."},{"label":"C","text":"They contain antigenic determinants."},{"label":"D","text":"They can elicit an immune response."},{"label":"E","text":"They contain paratopes. Antibodie detaminant"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9cf63a47cb18a77de7f62d18a35d6b8681410f09"},{"id":"q-31be9dc2811a","stem":"Central (primary) lymphoid organs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lymphoid organs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"are efficient in exposing T cells to foreign antigen."},{"label":"B","text":"are the primary site of antibody synthesis and release."},{"label":"C","text":"filter blood and trap blood-borne antigens."},{"label":"D","text":"provide the microenvironment for maturation of T and B cells."},{"label":"E","text":"line the mucosal surfaces of the body for efficient antigen contact."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"independent_checked","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"checked_explained","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a694ac2e73ac","stem":"Hematopoietic stem cells are pluripotent, which means that they are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen-specific cells."},{"label":"B","text":"capable of developing into any blood cells."},{"label":"C","text":"committed to produce cells of a single lineage."},{"label":"D","text":"not self-renewing."},{"label":"E","text":"T and B lymphocytes of many different antigen specificities."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb9b62d82700","stem":"Lymphocytes continually recirculate through peripheral lymphoid tissue in order to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"be killed before they cause autoimmunity."},{"label":"B","text":"efficiently encounter antigen."},{"label":"C","text":"mature from stem cells into lymphocytes."},{"label":"D","text":"phagocytose antigen and kill it."},{"label":"E","text":"go where no cell has gone before."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ffde49b752b","stem":"Peripheral lymphoid organs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"are centrally located in the abdomen to protect their vital functions."},{"label":"B","text":"are designed to maximize contact between antigen and lymphocytes."},{"label":"C","text":"produce antigen-specific lymphocytes from stem cells in response to antigen."},{"label":"D","text":"sequester antigen to minimize its damage to the body."},{"label":"E","text":"store large numbers of activated effector cells for a rapid response to antigen."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d22660a47a93","stem":"The PRIMARY purpose of the adaptive immune system is to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"block all pathogens from entering the body."},{"label":"B","text":"cure allergic reactions."},{"label":"C","text":"kill tumor cells."},{"label":"D","text":"protect from disease upon re-infection with a specific pathogen."},{"label":"E","text":"reject foreign transplants."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54ecdd3efb8d","stem":"Rapid but non-antigen specific immune responses are produced by the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"adaptive immune system."},{"label":"B","text":"innate immune system."},{"label":"C","text":"leukocytes."},{"label":"D","text":"lymphatic system."},{"label":"E","text":"memory response."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2bdb7e878912","stem":"Vaccination protects us from infectious disease by generating memory","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen."},{"label":"B","text":"lymphocytes."},{"label":"C","text":"macrophages."},{"label":"D","text":"PMNs."},{"label":"E","text":"stem cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ae451fd169b8","stem":"Which situation below describes an example of innate immunity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antibody production by plasma cells."},{"label":"B","text":"antigen removal by cilia in the respiratory tract."},{"label":"C","text":"complement activation by antibody bound to the surface of a bacterium."},{"label":"D","text":"memory response to influenza virus"},{"label":"E","text":"recognition and killing of virus-infected cells by cytotoxic T cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c1723273640","stem":"The antigen specificity of an adaptive immune response is due to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"activation of antigen-specific lymphocytes."},{"label":"B","text":"folding of antibody to fit the pathogen."},{"label":"C","text":"lysis of only certain pathogens by neutrophils."},{"label":"D","text":"phagocytosis of only certain pathogens by macrophages."},{"label":"E","text":"production of cytokines by antigen-specific macrophages"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-008f9ac7f110","stem":"Clonal selection","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"begins with inflammation."},{"label":"B","text":"occurs for all leukocytes."},{"label":"C","text":"occurs in response to self antigens."},{"label":"D","text":"results in innate immunity."},{"label":"E","text":"results in proliferation of antigen-specific lymphocytes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-afa806cbadd4","stem":"Cytokines are NOT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"able to induce increased blood vessel permeability."},{"label":"B","text":"antigen-specific."},{"label":"C","text":"made in response to bacterial antigens."},{"label":"D","text":"signals from one cell that affects the behavior of another cell."},{"label":"E","text":"secreted by macrophages."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d82a3c01991944d3fcd5f07e27922d4bc6c5afff"},{"id":"q-e0226132e948","stem":"A fundamental difference between the antigen receptors on B cells (BCR) and on T cells (TCR) is their","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"different requirements for antigen presentation."},{"label":"B","text":"function following antigen binding."},{"label":"C","text":"heterogeneity from one lymphocyte to the next."},{"label":"D","text":"heterogeneity on each lymphocyte."},{"label":"E","text":"membrane location."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30a533dec6f3","stem":"Genes for immunoglobulins (antibodies) are unlike other human genes in that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antibody genes are composed of introns and exons."},{"label":"B","text":"DNA for antibody molecules is inherited from only one parent."},{"label":"C","text":"