,{"label":"C","text":"hapten."},{"label":"D","text":"mitogen."},{"label":"E","text":"superantigen."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4c996d908a77","stem":"A polyclonal antibody response","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is not antigen-specific."},{"label":"B","text":"is produced only in response to polymeric antigens."},{"label":"C","text":"is produced by several B cells recognizing different epitopes on the same antigen."},{"label":"D","text":"occurs during the lag phase of the immune response."},{"label":"E","text":"violates clonal selection."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bf21cb185574","stem":"Very low doses of antigen may induce","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a secondary response."},{"label":"B","text":"hypersensitivity."},{"label":"C","text":"immunological ignorance."},{"label":"D","text":"low zone tolerance."},{"label":"E","text":"low zone immunity."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-424828cf7dbe","stem":"A virus vaccine that can activate cytotoxic T cells MUST contain","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a high dose of virus particles."},{"label":"B","text":"an adjuvant to stimulate T cell division."},{"label":"C","text":"foreign MHC."},{"label":"D","text":"live virus."},{"label":"E","text":"virus peptides."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88f068e1a3ac","stem":"Which statement about antigen epitopes is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An epitope may be shared by two different antigens."},{"label":"B","text":"A protein molecule usually contains multiple epitopes."},{"label":"C","text":"B cells bind only processed antigen epitopes."},{"label":"D","text":"Epitopes may be linear or assembled."},{"label":"E","text":"Some epitopes are more immunogenic than others"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c33d580be27c18fa6723d7f829b469338781b57","manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-b8d672be0b24","stem":"A patient desperately needs a bone marrow transplant, and a perfect match cannot be found. The rejection response in unmatched marrow is primarily due to the presence of mature T cells that recognize the recipient's cells as foreign. To minimize this rejection response, the marrow can be treated before transfusion into the recipient with complement plus antibody to human","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD3."},{"label":"B","text":"CD4."},{"label":"C","text":"CD8."},{"label":"D","text":"CD28."},{"label":"E","text":"CD154."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a0e0308299e9","stem":"Antibody to membrane receptors sometimes inhibits receptor function and sometimes mimics the action of the normal receptor ligand. (For example, some antibodies to insulin receptor block the action of insulin and some mimic the action of insulin.) An antibody which should NOT either block or stimulate B cell function would be anti","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD21."},{"label":"B","text":"CD56."},{"label":"C","text":"CD80."},{"label":"D","text":"Igα."},{"label":"E","text":"μ chain. CYTOKINES QUESTIONES!!!!!! Pick the one BEST answer for each question by clicking on the letter of the correct choice."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1a300f729c78","stem":"Cytokines may exhibit __________ action, signaling the cells that produce them.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antagonistic"},{"label":"B","text":"autocrine"},{"label":"C","text":"endocrine"},{"label":"D","text":"paracrine."},{"label":"E","text":"synergistic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-0bbbbcc9d13c","stem":"Cytokines are NOT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen specific."},{"label":"B","text":"capable of activating more than one cell type."},{"label":"C","text":"made by lymphocytes."},{"label":"D","text":"small protein molecules."},{"label":"E","text":"synthesized de novo in response to antigen or other cytokines."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d82a3c01991944d3fcd5f07e27922d4bc6c5afff"},{"id":"q-235646231ded","stem":"Several cytokines may have the same effect on the cells they bind. This is an example of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a cascade."},{"label":"B","text":"antagonism."},{"label":"C","text":"pleiotropism."},{"label":"D","text":"redundancy."},{"label":"E","text":"synergy."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a6d450af0911","stem":"Characterization of cytokine activities is NOT made more difficult by their","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"gene structure."},{"label":"B","text":"pleiotropism."},{"label":"C","text":"redundancy."},{"label":"D","text":"secretion close to target cell membranes."},{"label":"E","text":"short half-lives."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-63ccf8de801a","stem":"A cytokine can do all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bind to receptors which do not share cytokine-binding subunits."},{"label":"B","text":"bind to its specific receptor on the same cell that produced it."},{"label":"C","text":"bind to receptor antagonists produced by pathogenic viruses."},{"label":"D","text":"compete with other cytokines whose receptors share signal-transducing subunits"},{"label":"E","text":"upregulate (increase) synthesis of high affinity subunits for its receptor."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d911ecd81208","stem":"Members of a cytokine receptor family","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"all bind the same cytokines."},{"label":"B","text":"are grouped together because they share antigen specificity"},{"label":"C","text":"are often found on the same cells"},{"label":"D","text":"are similar in protein structure and sometimes in regions of amino acid sequence."},{"label":"E","text":"are specific for cytokines produced by a single cell type"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0b6b1701bef","stem":"The ability of a cytokine to change gene expression in the target cell is influenced by all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"presence of high-affinity receptors on the target cell."},{"label":"B","text":"presence of soluble cytokine receptors."},{"label":"C","text":"proximity of the producing and target cells."},{"label":"D","text":"rate of transport of cytokine-receptor complexes into the cytoplasm."},{"label":"E","text":"simultaneous production of another cytokine whose receptor uses the same signal transducing subunit."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-96e64847c891","stem":"Cytokines are NOT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"able to inhibit the function of other cytokines."},{"label":"B","text":"able to stimulate the synthesis of other cytokines."},{"label":"C","text":"produced by more than one cell type."},{"label":"D","text":"small protein molecules."},{"label":"E","text":"stored in the cell for quick release."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d82a3c01991944d3fcd5f07e27922d4bc6c5afff"},{"id":"q-ea54b5dbe59c","stem":"The IL-2R subfamily consists of receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, and IL-15. This group of cytokine receptors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bind all five cytokines to promote synergistic action on target cells."},{"label":"B","text":"bind cytokines which are produced by the same cell."},{"label":"C","text":"each has a unique high affinity cytokine-specific α chain."},{"label":"D","text":"shift the immune response towards cellular immunity."},{"label":"E","text":"each has a unique signal-transducing γ chain."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1973a5249f87","stem":"An antagonist for cytokine X may NOT be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cytokine A competing for a shared receptor subunit."},{"label":"B","text":"cytokine B which acts synergistically with cytokine X."},{"label":"C","text":"cytokine C which inhibits the activation of the cell that produces cytokine X."},{"label":"D","text":"made by microorganisms."},{"label":"E","text":"soluble cytokine X receptors."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c2791da39e4b","stem":"A knock-out mouse for a particular cytokine allows immunologists to characterize cytokine function","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"by doing a dose-response study with competing cytokines."},{"label":"B","text":"in the absence of all other cytokines."},{"label":"C","text":"on all cell types simultaneously."},{"label":"D","text":"under controlled conditions of local cytokine concentrations."},{"label":"E","text":"with defined cell populations."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bfef69f763e8","stem":"Activated Tc can regulate immune responses by signaling activated lymphocytes to undergo","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"apoptosis."},{"label":"B","text":"clonal deletion."},{"label":"C","text":"clonal proliferation."},{"label":"D","text":"cytotoxicity."},{"label":"E","text":"somatic hypermutation. QUESTIONS ON COMPLEMENT:-"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e33f9e3b44a","stem":"Complement is involved in all of the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"attraction of neutrophils to an infection site."},{"label":"B","text":"increased presence of serum proteins in the infected tissues."},{"label":"C","text":"lysis of bacteria in the absence of specific antibodies."},{"label":"D","text":"opsonization of microorganisms for phagocytosis."},{"label":"E","text":"sensitization of T cells to antigen"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f33833c9664","stem":"Complement is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"activated by binding to specific complement receptors."},{"label":"B","text":"antigen-specific."},{"label":"C","text":"a potent promoter of virus entry into host cells."},{"label":"D","text":"a series of intracellular proteins which work with antibody to eliminate endogenous antigen."},{"label":"E","text":"present in the circulation in an inactive form."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f4323bde4743","stem":"The alternative pathway of complement activation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"causes tissue damage in the absence of C1INH."},{"label":"B","text":"occurs after the classic pathway is activated."},{"label":"C","text":"occurs only if the classical pathway is ineffective in pathogen clearance."},{"label":"D","text":"requires C3."},{"label":"E","text":"requires C4."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-241b2e9ea3f2","stem":"If a person is born without C2 and C4","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"C5 can still be cleaved by the classical pathway."},{"label":"B","text":"C3b will not be able to bind to bacteria."},{"label":"C","text":"C9 will polymerize inappropriately and lyse host cells."},{"label":"D","text":"the classical pathway will be changed into the alternative pathway."},{"label":"E","text":"the amount of C3b produced during bacterial infections will be reduced."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-719d5606a8b2","stem":"Which of the following are least sensitive to complement-mediated lysis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enveloped viruses"},{"label":"B","text":"Erythrocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Gram negative bacteria"},{"label":"D","text":"Gram positive bacteria"},{"label":"E","text":"Leukocytes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76f2cd3a00eb","stem":"In the membrane attack phase of the classical complement pathway, the role of C5b is to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"activate the C5 convertase activity."},{"label":"B","text":"attract neutrophils to lyse the pathogen."},{"label":"C","text":"initiate formation of the MAC."},{"label":"D","text":"polymerize into a membrane-spanning channel."},{"label":"E","text":"All of these are activities of C5b."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ddaddd41440","stem":"Complement receptors (CR)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"activate complement on the surface of pathogens."},{"label":"B","text":"bind only activated complement proteins."},{"label":"C","text":"inhibit complement activation on the surface of host cells."},{"label":"D","text":"on erythrocytes remove immune complexes from the circulation."},{"label":"E","text":"on macrophages signal host cells to make opsonins."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e3499b264d88","stem":"As complement is activated by complexes of antibody-coated bacteria, bystander lysis of nearby host cells is prevented by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a long-lived thioester bond on active complement proteins."},{"label":"B","text":"covalent attachment of all active complement proteins to the pathogen surface."},{"label":"C","text":"plasma proteins that inactivate the anaphylatoxins."},{"label":"D","text":"proteins on host cell membranes that inhibit MAC formation."},{"label":"E","text":"the slow catalytic rates of complement proteases."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-512132584cc8","stem":"Complement activity is restricted by all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"dissociation of C3 and C5 convertases."},{"label":"B","text":"Gram positive cell walls that are resistant to MAC polymerization."},{"label":"C","text":"host cell plasma proteins that inactivate C3a, C4a, and C5a activity."},{"label":"D","text":"LPS in the outer membrane of Gram negative bacteria that inactivates C3b."},{"label":"E","text":"proteolytic cleavage of complement proteins into smaller fragments."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7532800a2706","stem":"A deficiency in complement proteins or in their regulators can result in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"blood in the urine from erythrocyte lysis."},{"label":"B","text":"decreased levels of certain complement proteins in the circulation."},{"label":"C","text":"immune complex disease."},{"label":"D","text":"increased numbers of infections."},{"label":"E","text":"All of the above can result from complement deficiencies. Problem Complete the table below by predicting the effects of a complete deficiency of each of the complement proteins listed at the top of the chart on the activities shown in the left hand column. Use the notation N = no inhibition; P = partial inhibition; C = complete inhibition. | |C1 | |-Pathogen binding by phagocyte |-Microbe is covered with capsule or host proteins that do not bind | |-Phagocytosis |macrophage receptors. | |-Pathogen elimination |-Microbe has surface molecules that inhibit complement activation. | |-Inflammation |-Microbe is Gram positive (has thick peptidoglycan cell wall resistant to | |-Macrophage cytokine secretion |insertion of poly C9). | |-CAM expression |-Microbe blocks fusion of lysosome with phagosome. | |-Vascular leakiness |-Microbe is resistant to killing molecules in phagolysosome. | |-Neutrophil extravasation |-Microbe is a virus that lives in cell cytoplasm. | |-Acute phase protein synthesis |-Microbe remains on mucosal cell surface but produces a toxin. | |-Acute phase protein function |-Microbe suppresses infected cell expression of MHC. | |-Complement activation |-Host is deficient in C3 or C3R. | |-Complement-mediated lysis |-Host is deficient in phagocyte NADP oxidase. | |-Interferon production |-Host is deficient in ICAM-1. | |-Interferon function |-Host cells have a defective IL-1 receptor. (IL-6. IL-8. IL-12. TNFα). | |-NK cell function |-Host has a defective mannan-binding lectin (MBL) or LPS-binding protein | | |(LBP). |"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8268bbfb1a0c","stem":"An antibody Fab contains","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"complementarity determining regions."},{"label":"B","text":"H and L chain variable regions."},{"label":"C","text":"one antigen binding region."},{"label":"D","text":"one H-L interchain disulfide bond."},{"label":"E","text":"all of the above."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f0fe97ba4183","stem":"Myeloma proteins are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"abnormally formed antibodies secreted from cancerous plasma cells."},{"label":"B","text":"cancerous plasma cells that divide without requiring antigen activation."},{"label":"C","text":"cell lines that secrete specific antibodies for a short time, then die."},{"label":"D","text":"homogeneous antibody molecules secreted by plasma cell tumors."},{"label":"E","text":"protein signaling molecules that make a plasma cell become a multiple myeloma."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f4e82a71ece5","stem":"The regions of the antibody molecule which contribute MOST to the affinity of the antibody for antigen are the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CDR."},{"label":"B","text":"Fab regions."},{"label":"C","text":"Fc regions."},{"label":"D","text":"framework regions."},{"label":"E","text":"hinge regions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-98b18b67b662","stem":"Antibody Fc fragments contain","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen-binding sites."},{"label":"B","text":"CDR."},{"label":"C","text":"complement-binding sites."},{"label":"D","text":"framework residues."},{"label":"E","text":"light chain variable domains."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3268bd54cf79","stem":"The immunoglobulin isotype is determined by the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen specificity."},{"label":"B","text":"H chain constant region."},{"label":"C","text":"L chain variable region."},{"label":"D","text":"number of antigen-binding sites."},{"label":"E","text":"number of VH domains."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a1ad1e36b48a","stem":"Which statement about antigen epitopes is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An epitope may be shared by two different antigens."},{"label":"B","text":"A protein molecule usually contains multiple epitopes."},{"label":"C","text":"B cells bind only processed antigen epitopes."},{"label":"D","text":"Epitopes may be linear (composed of sequential amino acids) or assembled by protein folding from amino acids far apart in the protein primary amino acid sequence."},{"label":"E","text":"Some epitopes are more immunogenic than others."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c33d580be27c18fa6723d7f829b469338781b57"},{"id":"q-093c3e975d10","stem":"An example of an antigen epitope from an infectious organism would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a bacterial endotoxin (LPS) molecule."},{"label":"B","text":"a fungal cell wall protein."},{"label":"C","text":"a peptide on the surface of a virus capsid protein."},{"label":"D","text":"a whole virus."},{"label":"E","text":"All of the above are antigen epitopes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-230e2237c062","stem":"Antibody affinity for antigen depends on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the antibody isotype."},{"label":"B","text":"the complementary shape and charge of each antibody V region for its antigen epitope."},{"label":"C","text":"the number of Fab regions in each antibody molecule."},{"label":"D","text":"whether the antibody is in the serum or on the cell surface."},{"label":"E","text":"whether the light chains are kappa or lambda."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1203d3b959c9","stem":"A colleague sends you an antibody to polio virus capsid protein. You perform equilibrium dialysis on the antibody to measure its affinity. Plotting r/c versus r gives you a curved line with K= 2.5 X 108 L/mole and an r intercept of 4. From these results, you conclude that the antibody is probably","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a cross-reactive antibody."},{"label":"B","text":"a monoclonal anti-polio virus antibody."},{"label":"C","text":"a polyclonal IgG antibody."},{"label":"D","text":"IgA anti-polio virus."},{"label":"E","text":"not specific for polio virus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c3233261466f","stem":"Allotypic determinants are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"constant region determinants that distinguish each Ig class and subclass within a species."},{"label":"B","text":"expressed only from the paternal chromosome."},{"label":"C","text":"generated by the conformation of antigen-specific VH and VL sequences."},{"label":"D","text":"Not immunogenic in individuals who do not have that allotype."},{"label":"E","text":"amino acid differences encoded by different alleles for the same H or L chain locus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b4dadcfb9e7","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of IgG?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It contains 2 γ and 2 L chains"},{"label":"B","text":"It crosses the placenta."},{"label":"C","text":"It is the predominant immunoglobulin in blood, lymph, and peritoneal fluid."},{"label":"D","text":"It is the largest of all the Igs."},{"label":"E","text":"Its L chains are either κ or λ."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a11cefe90ffe","stem":"Human serum IgA is isolated and injected into a rabbit. The rabbit anti-IgA antibodies will react against all of the following EXCEPT human","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α chain."},{"label":"B","text":"IgG."},{"label":"C","text":"κ chain."},{"label":"D","text":"λ chain."},{"label":"E","text":"secretory component."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b26079d487f8","stem":"You have purified some Fab from an IgG myeloma protein. Under appropriate conditions, you could use this Fab to generate antibodies to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"both κ and λ chain."},{"label":"B","text":"γ chain hinge region."},{"label":"C","text":"J chain."},{"label":"D","text":"γ chain allotypic determinants."},{"label":"E","text":"the idiotype of this myeloma."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e6f82ad07a97","stem":"The Ig isotype which would be most important for neutralizing polio virus before it could infect intestinal cells would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":"Mucosal immunology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"secretory IgA."},{"label":"B","text":"serum IgA."},{"label":"C","text":"serum IgD."},{"label":"D","text":"serum IgG."},{"label":"E","text":"membrane IgM."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_checked","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"checked_explained","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a841e91359a","stem":"Which of the following changes to a serum IgM antibody molecule would definitely DECREASE its avidity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase noncovalent antigen-antibody interactions in the CDR."},{"label":"B","text":"Remove the secretory component."},{"label":"C","text":"Replace the Fc portion of the mu chains with the Fc portion of alpha chains."},{"label":"D","text":"Replace VH and VL framework regions with those from a different antibody."},{"label":"E","text":"Use limited enzyme digestion to make Fab fragments."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-928a5225e5f9","stem":"IgA can be secreted from the body because it","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"binds poly-Ig receptor on mucosal epithelial cells."},{"label":"B","text":"has a specialized H chain called secretory chain."},{"label":"C","text":"has a special secretory idiotype."},{"label":"D","text":"is small enough to pass between mucosal epithelial cells and leave the body."},{"label":"E","text":"is synthesized by mucosal epithelial cells and secreted directly into the intestinal lumen."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e09c88b849df","stem":"The ability to make antibody with the same antigen specificity but different Fc regions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"causes allelic exclusion of Ig molecules."},{"label":"B","text":"does not occur against bacterial antigens."},{"label":"C","text":"improves the antigen binding specificity of an Ig molecule."},{"label":"D","text":"increases the effector functions of Ig molecules."},{"label":"E","text":"requires clonal elimination."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27c99ab33315","stem":"Allergy symptoms are produced when antigen binds to IgE on FcR on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A cells."},{"label":"B","text":"macrophages."},{"label":"C","text":"mast cells."},{"label":"D","text":"neutrophils."},{"label":"E","text":"Th1 cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fab8d7c601e4","stem":"One amino acid difference in the Fc region of different human γ chains is the epitope recognized by anti","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"allotype."},{"label":"B","text":"idiotype."},{"label":"C","text":"isotype."},{"label":"D","text":"IgG."},{"label":"E","text":"γ chain. Problems"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24b5674e7ccc","stem":"You inject a rabbit on several occasions with pure human serum albumin (HSA), collect some blood from the rabbit and allow it to clot. The yellowish liquid remaining is your antiserum. check your answer? data?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Why did you give several injections of antigen? How long would you wait after the last one before collecting blood?"},{"label":"B","text":"Are all the molecules in the antiserum antibodies? Are all the antibody molecules in the serum specific for HSA? How can you"},{"label":"C","text":"How can you separate the anti-HSA from the other molecules (purify it)? After purification, you measure the binding of your antiserum to human serum albumin (HSA), human immunoglobulin (HIg), rabbit serum albumin (RSA), and chimpanzee serum albumin (CSA). The results are shown in the table below: |Antigen |Binding units | |HSA |100 | |HIg |1 | |RSA |1 | |CSA |90 |"},{"label":"D","text":"Why should an antibody to HSA bind CSA? What can you deduce about the structures of these four molecules from the binding"},{"label":"E","text":"From amino acid sequencing, HSA and CSA are about 90% homologous (have the same amino acids at about 90% of the sequence) and HSA and RSA are about 45% homologous. Why does the antiserum show so little binding to RSA? Are all epitopes of an antigen equally immunogenic? Predict the results if the antiserum had been made in a chimp."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af0f5b8d4795","stem":"For each antiserum below, what would you use as an antigen and what animal would you inject to produce antibody?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-mouse α chain isotype?"},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-mouse chain allotype?"},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-mouse (anti-HEL) idiotype QSTN ON Ab-genes:-"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-55302dbe9561","stem":"Genes for immunoglobulins are unlike other human genes in that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"each polypeptide chain is encoded by several exons."},{"label":"B","text":"Ig genes are composed of introns and exons"},{"label":"C","text":"somatic recombination occurs before mRNA is transcribed"},{"label":"D","text":"there is less Ig genetic material in mature B cells than in other somatic cells"},{"label":"E","text":"both c and d are true."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-b427af6bd24c","stem":"The gene segments needed to encode the variable region of a κ chain are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"one Jκ plus one Dκ."},{"label":"B","text":"one Jκ plus one Cκ."},{"label":"C","text":"one Vκ plus one Dκ."},{"label":"D","text":"one Vκ plus one Jκ."},{"label":"E","text":"one Vκ plus one Jκ plus one Dκ."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-49a03a24059d","stem":"Pseudogenes are DNA sequences which look very similar to functional genes except for the presence of a(n)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"intron."},{"label":"B","text":"leader sequence."},{"label":"C","text":"promoter codon."},{"label":"D","text":"signal sequence."},{"label":"E","text":"stop codon."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f28c0e1dea8a","stem":"Combinatorial diversity says that by random combination of 40 functional Vκ segments with five Jκ segments, the number of possible different κ chains that could be made are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"40."},{"label":"B","text":"45."},{"label":"C","text":"70."},{"label":"D","text":"200."},{"label":"E","text":"1200."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ac07c786299d","stem":"Which does NOT contribute to Ig antigen-binding diversity","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Any L chain can combine with any H chain to form a functional antibody."},{"label":"B","text":"Any Vκ can be joined to any Jκ to encode the light chain V region."},{"label":"C","text":"Many CH genes are present in the germline DNA."},{"label":"D","text":"Random numbers of N nucleotides can be added during somatic recombination."},{"label":"E","text":"VJL and VDJH joining is imprecise."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c8c99a4d2ab","stem":"The proper joining of one VL to one JL is regulated by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"heptamer and nonamer sequences."},{"label":"B","text":"leader sequences."},{"label":"C","text":"P-nucleotide addition sites."},{"label":"D","text":"12 and 23 nucleotide spacers between heptamer and nonamer sequences."},{"label":"E","text":"TdT binding site for DNA."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb7498c326f0","stem":"Since each B cell productively rearranges a single H and L chain allele, it exhibits","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"affinity."},{"label":"B","text":"allelic exclusion"},{"label":"C","text":"antibody restriction."},{"label":"D","text":"antigen-binding diversity."},{"label":"E","text":"cross-reactivity"}],"priority":0,"stat