D, K, and L."},{"label":"C","text":"H-2 IA and IE"},{"label":"D","text":"HLA-A. -B, and -C."},{"label":"E","text":"HLA-DR, -DP, and -DQ."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3e2c868a6d5e","stem":"Cells which have MHC Class II are _________________, which present _____________ antigen to Th cells.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen presenting cells, endogenous"},{"label":"B","text":"antigen presenting cells, exogenous"},{"label":"C","text":"infected cells, inflammatory"},{"label":"D","text":"target cells, endogenous"},{"label":"E","text":"target cells, exogenous"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7056e02ccfc4","stem":"Signaling to a cytotoxic T cell that a liver cell is infected with hepatitis virus depends on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"binding of Ii to Class I MHC until the peptide is loaded."},{"label":"B","text":"binding of TCR on the cytotoxic T cell to Class II MHC on the infected cell."},{"label":"C","text":"binding of processed antigen to liver cell Class I MHC."},{"label":"D","text":"processing the hepatitis virus peptides to the correct size and anchor residues in the endosomal pathway."},{"label":"E","text":"both c and d are correct."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-6ccf935cd6ff","stem":"Endogenous antigen presentation requires delivery of antigen peptides to the endoplasmic reticulum by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Class I MHC and invariant chain."},{"label":"B","text":"calnexin and tapasin."},{"label":"C","text":"HLA-DM."},{"label":"D","text":"leader sequence."},{"label":"E","text":"TAP-1 and TAP-2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9573eb1dcbbb","stem":"Following virus infection, peptides produced from the proteasome are more likely to be presented on the surface of the target cell because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"MHC Class I is synthesized in response to virus infection."},{"label":"B","text":"proteasomal enzymes which produce shorter peptides are synthesized in response to virus infection."},{"label":"C","text":"TAP-1 and TAP-2 specifically bind virus peptides."},{"label":"D","text":"virus amino and carboxyl terminal amino acids bind better to Class I MHC than peptides from self proteins."},{"label":"E","text":"virus infection induces expression of proteases which cut proteins at sites which bind best to TAP-1 and TAP-2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0eeafc37092e","stem":"Exogenous antigen is processed","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"after presentation by antigen presenting cells."},{"label":"B","text":"by nearly every nucleated cell."},{"label":"C","text":"by the cytosolic processing pathway."},{"label":"D","text":"in the presence of β2-microglobulin."},{"label":"E","text":"in acidified endosomes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be466a891254","stem":"Class II MHC does not efficiently present endogenous antigen because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen synthesized inside the cell never makes it to the endosomal compartment."},{"label":"B","text":"endogenous antigen cannot be processed into peptides small enough."},{"label":"C","text":"HLA DM transports Class II to the surface before it can bind endogenous peptide."},{"label":"D","text":"invariant chain blocks binding of endogenous peptide in the ER."},{"label":"E","text":"phagocytosed antigen binds Class II as rapidly as Class II is synthesized."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7cbe01b8a82b","stem":"MIIC is a specialized intracellular compartment where","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA DM promotes the release of CLIP and peptide binding to Class II MHC."},{"label":"B","text":"invariant chain binds to Class II MHC α and β chains."},{"label":"C","text":"peptides are transported into the ER for binding to Class II."},{"label":"D","text":"proteins are broken down into peptides by proteasomes."},{"label":"E","text":"some pathogens live protected from lysosomal enzymes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0447ce70b39b","stem":"In order to have pathogen peptide plus Class II MHC molecules expressed on the membrane of host cells, all of the following are required EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"β2-microglobulin."},{"label":"B","text":"CLIP."},{"label":"C","text":"HLA-DM."},{"label":"D","text":"HLA-DR, -DP, and -DQ alpha chains."},{"label":"E","text":"Ii ."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40d3df0df30c","stem":"Invariant chain (Ii)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"inhibits binding of endogenous peptide to Class I MHC."},{"label":"B","text":"is degraded in the MIIC compartment to CLIP."},{"label":"C","text":"is released from Class II upon binding of β2-microglobulin."},{"label":"D","text":"is the constant region of Class I peptide binding site."},{"label":"E","text":"prevents exogenous peptide binding to Class II MHC in the ER."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e67d09123430","stem":"Antigen binding by Class I MHC molecules","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"accommodates many different peptides."},{"label":"B","text":"preferentially occurs for peptides 13-18 amino acids in length."},{"label":"C","text":"occurs at a site on Class I MHC formed by folding of α1 and β2-microglobulin domains."},{"label":"D","text":"occurs only on antigen presenting cells."},{"label":"E","text":"takes place at the plasma membrane of the infected cell."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bf1471f4d921","stem":"Both Class I and Class II MHC molecules are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"composed of α and β chains with variable and constant regions."},{"label":"B","text":"expressed constitutively on all nucleated cells."},{"label":"C","text":"expressed on the B cell membrane."},{"label":"D","text":"part of the T cell receptor for antigen."},{"label":"E","text":"synthesized in response to antigen processing."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a84e824d1b8b","stem":"The major histocompatibility complex has","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"dozens of loci for Class I and Class II proteins."},{"label":"B","text":"genes that encode proteins associated with antigen processing."},{"label":"C","text":"only genes encoding Class I and Class II molecules."},{"label":"D","text":"single loci for Class I and Class II proteins."},{"label":"E","text":"three regions encoding Class I, Class II, and Class III receptors."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-74f38b9fc048","stem":"MHC polymorphism","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is generated by recombination of HLA A, B, and C gene segments."},{"label":"B","text":"is present primarily in the peptide-binding regions of MHC proteins."},{"label":"C","text":"is the result of random association of many alpha and beta genes."},{"label":"D","text":"restricts the ability of B cells to bind antigen."},{"label":"E","text":"results in expression of dozens of MHC alleles on each APC."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5a576218d45","stem":"T cells are MHC-restricted in their ability to respond to antigen because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"all antigen must be processed and presented to activate lymphocytes."},{"label":"B","text":"during an infection, all cells in the body present antigen on MHC Class I."},{"label":"C","text":"MHC binds antigen more specifically than TCR does."},{"label":"D","text":"TCR must recognize both antigen and MHC molecules."},{"label":"E","text":"the T cells should not respond to antigen on allogeneic cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a5e7950d174c","stem":"Linkage of a disease to an HLA allele means that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"everyone with that allele will eventually get the disease."},{"label":"B","text":"people with that allele have a higher risk for the disease."},{"label":"C","text":"the MHC protein encoded by that allele is defective."},{"label":"D","text":"the allele will eventually disappear from the population."},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above is true."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d7375cb7fe7","stem":"All of the following are associated with the expression of Class I MHC molecules EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antigen peptide presentation on membrane Class I MHC to Tc."},{"label":"B","text":"graft rejection."},{"label":"C","text":"increased risk of certain autoimmune diseases."},{"label":"D","text":"lysis of virus-infected cells."},{"label":"E","text":"stimulation of antibody production."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-07087cd6b5cb","stem":"Human Class II MHC molecules","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"are encoded by the genes HLA-A, B, and C."},{"label":"B","text":"are found on all nucleated cells."},{"label":"C","text":"have an antigen binding site formed from regions of two polypeptide chains."},{"label":"D","text":"must be associated with β2-microglobulin molecules to bind peptide."},{"label":"E","text":"present antigen to CD8 cytotoxic T cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-101eb4432471","stem":"Humans inherit from each of their parents","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a random set of MHC Class I, Class II, and Class III genes."},{"label":"B","text":"enough diversity in MHC to present epitopes from most pathogens."},{"label":"C","text":"enough diversity in MHC to present every possible antigen epitope."},{"label":"D","text":"genes for α and β chains that can be recombined to increase their diversity."},{"label":"E","text":"the same Class I and Class II MHC genes as their siblings."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0c4591342693","stem":"The α chain of HLA-DR","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"can be expressed with the β chain of any MHC molecule."},{"label":"B","text":"can be expressed with the β chain of any Class II MHC molecule."},{"label":"C","text":"can be expressed with the β chain of any Class II DR molecule."},{"label":"D","text":"must be expressed with β2-microglobulin."},{"label":"E","text":"must be expressed with the β chain of Class II DR from the same chromosome."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9102cb080c39","stem":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Each individual expresses all the diversity of MHC protein structure."},{"label":"B","text":"If a family has four children, no two of them will have the same MHC genotype."},{"label":"C","text":"Someone with bare lymphocyte syndrome who expressed no MHC proteins would die in infancy."},{"label":"D","text":"TCR on Tc cells binds α1 and β2 domains of Class I MHC protein."},{"label":"E","text":"The chances of finding a tissue match are much higher between children and their parents than between siblings."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"248a6c54f8445b675f896816d3de20b31c35fe86"},{"id":"q-61e967fd70f3","stem":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All MHC alleles in the population have been counted."},{"label":"B","text":"CD4 T cells see antigen on self Class II MHC but not on self Class I MHC."},{"label":"C","text":"Human Class II MHC proteins are called HLA DP, HLA DQ, and HLA DR."},{"label":"D","text":"Class I and Class II MHC are less antigen-specific than Ig."},{"label":"E","text":"Peptides presented by Class I MHC must be 8-10 amino acids long."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddd53843a7c3208057acb162a2c5585b42e5b16c","manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-5899e292a38f","stem":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A peptide binding to Class I must have certain amino- and carboxyl-terminal amino acids to bind tightly to the ends of the Class I binding cleft."},{"label":"B","text":"A transplant is most likely to be successful between people who share the same alleles at all Class I and Class II MHC loci."},{"label":"C","text":"Identical twins share all their Class I and Class II MHC alleles."},{"label":"D","text":"Peptide binding to TCR is influenced by both its own conformation and the conformation of the MHC protein to which it is bound."},{"label":"E","text":"The gene for β2-microglobulin is in the Class I region of the MHC. Problems"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddd53843a7c3208057acb162a2c5585b42e5b16c"},{"id":"q-b291d895c454","stem":"What is the maximum number of Class I and Class II proteins expressed on a liver cell (hepatocyte)? on a macrophage?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ef2b4b89a26","stem":"Describe antigen processing and presentation of antigens from Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) which has infected B cells.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5e7f62abc787","stem":"Influenza virus is an enveloped virus that infects primarily respiratory epithelial cells, but during the course of the infection respiratory tract DC also become infected and carry the virus to nearby lymphoid tissue, where they present it to lymphocytes. Describe how the virus would be processed and presented by these infected dendritic cells. To what cells would they present the antigens? What factors will influence how efficiently the antigens are presented?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Virology","topicOriginal":"Virology","subtopic":"Viruses","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Virology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-764f7ae5af8e","stem":"In order to study MHC restriction of T cells, you make two clones of mouse CD8 Tc cells. The first is specific for peptide A presented on H-2k, and the second is specific for peptide B presented on H-2d. You then transfect cDNA for TCR from the cells of the first clone into cells from the second clone; the transfected T cells express both TCR molecules on each cell. Which of the following target cells would be killed by the transfected T cell clone and why?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Microbiology","to