cationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42b4eff5009f","stem":"To interpret the relationship between two categorical variables, a contingency table should be constructed with either column or row percentages, and ----.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"If the percentages are calculated down the columns, then comparisons should be made across the rows"},{"label":"B","text":"If the percentages are calculated across the rows, comparisons should be made down the columns"},{"label":"C","text":"Both a and b are correct"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither a nor b is correct Answers:"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e7800145c5cc","stem":"Why is it important to determine if a variable is normally distributed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it changes the way you summarize data"},{"label":"B","text":"it changes the way you analyse your data"},{"label":"C","text":"it is absolutely irrelevant"},{"label":"D","text":"both b and c"},{"label":"E","text":"all are correct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"28cb5ac4b3c10ecdb1b9430fae07d6ceddc50761"},{"id":"q-18673e607442","stem":"Consider the values below and choose the best summary of the age-data below 15, 17, 12, 16, 48","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the mean of this group is 21.6 year (sd 14.9)"},{"label":"B","text":"the median of this group is 16 (interquartile range 15-17 years) If it is squde the median is calculated but not mean."},{"label":"C","text":"the range of this group is 12 – 48 years"},{"label":"D","text":"the mean of this group the mean of this group is 21.6 year (sd 14.9); median 16 years (interquartile range 15-17 years)"},{"label":"E","text":"the mean is equal t the median"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ecf9f1bfbdfe9b0f7aed48860ede171853bf414e"},{"id":"q-72cd43c20c7b","stem":"What options do you have to summarize data which is not normally distributed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"give the population mean"},{"label":"B","text":"give the population median"},{"label":"C","text":"give the population mode"},{"label":"D","text":"answers b and c are correct"},{"label":"E","text":"answers a, b and c are correct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40551917c68c","stem":"What is the correct description of a 95% confidence interval around a mean?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The range in which 95% of the observations fall"},{"label":"B","text":"The range that is given by two times the standard deviation"},{"label":"C","text":"The range around your estimate of the mean that holds the true population value of the mean with 95% confidence"},{"label":"D","text":"It gives correct estimates of the mean 95% of the times"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"beba58e70a2b5f5c0a89a8a3357570d3070e67c1"},{"id":"q-ced4186f199d","stem":"For the following histogram, what is the proper ordering of the mean, Median and mode? Note that the graph is NOT numerically precise only the relative positions are important. [pic] (left skewed median is greater than mean and visa vi)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"I = mean II = median III = mode"},{"label":"B","text":"I =mode II = median III = mean"},{"label":"C","text":"I = median II = mean III = mode"},{"label":"D","text":"I = mode II = mean III = median"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dfa54ff4efc1","stem":"The standard error of the mean is: (Select the best statement.)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"used to calculate the mean of the sample."},{"label":"B","text":"used whenever the researcher wants to adjust for confounding by the direct or indirect standardization method."},{"label":"C","text":"the sample size divided by the square root of the sample standard deviation."},{"label":"D","text":"a type of sampling bias that can lead to an erroneous study conclusion."},{"label":"E","text":"the standard deviation of the distribution of sample means."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2bc5a3ce542f","stem":"Select the best statement concerning a normal (Gaussian) distribution","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The variance equals the standard deviation."},{"label":"B","text":"The probability of randomly selecting a variable higher than plus or minus two standard deviations above the mean is 5%."},{"label":"C","text":"The probability of randomly selecting a variable higher than one standard deviation above the mean is 16%."},{"label":"D","text":"The total area under the curve cannot be calculated unless the mean, median and standard deviation are known."},{"label":"E","text":"The distribution is symmetric about each standard deviation."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-027daa0a51e4","stem":"The power of statistical test can be determined by using the formula","type":"Calculation","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1- H0"},{"label":"B","text":"1- HA"},{"label":"C","text":"1- ( 20%"},{"label":"D","text":"1- ("},{"label":"E","text":"H0-HA"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb3c36f533bd","stem":"The grades on exam C are normally distributed with a population mean of 75% and a population standard deviation of 5%. Select the best statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The 95% confidence interval on the exam C distribution extends from 65% to 85%."},{"label":"B","text":"34% of the grades on exam C are between 75% and 80%."},{"label":