bject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e1fe015be57a","stem":"Regarding incidence risk which is false?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a measure of risk of developing disease over specified period"},{"label":"B","text":"Is estimated from dynamic cohort or open cohort"},{"label":"C","text":"Measure probability of an event happening over specified time"},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as cumulative incidence Use the information to answer question 62 – 63: A population of 100 healthy men in Moshi was followed for 10 years to assess the development of prostate cancer. Fifteen men were lost after one year and 30 men developed prostate cancer after 5 years of follow up, the remaining men never developed the disease for the whole duration of the study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ebe24dd51b6bea519c12a6b56fe0afe86ec7a664"},{"id":"q-5b1d7da16848","stem":"The number of person-years of observation in this study was","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"810"},{"label":"B","text":"715"},{"label":"C","text":"815"},{"label":"D","text":"1000"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b56d8e57d64","stem":"The incidence rate in this study was","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"41.9 per 1,000 PYRs"},{"label":"B","text":"37.0 per 1,000 PYRs"},{"label":"C","text":"30.0 per 1,000 PYRs"},{"label":"D","text":"24.7 per 1,000 PYRs"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5fd64b4a46b","stem":"The mortality rate due to disease X in city A is 75/100,000 in persons aged 65-69 years old. The mortality rate due to the same disease in city B is 150/100,000 in persons aged 65-69 years old. The inference that disease X is two times more prevalent in persons 65-69 years old in city B than t is in persons 65-69 years old in city A is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Correct"},{"label":"B","text":"Incorrect, because of failure to distinguish between prevalence and mortality"},{"label":"C","text":"Incorrect, because of failure to distinguish between period and point prevalence"},{"label":"D","text":"Incorrect, because a proportion is used when a rate is required to support inference"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-886f106ace7a","stem":"The incidence and prevalence rates of disease X in children under five from 1983-1992, is depicted in the figure below. [pic] The best explanation of the graph could be due to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In migration of non diseased people"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased mortality in diseased people"},{"label":"C","text":"Improved survival of diseased people"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased incidence of the disease"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-255ee8ba6813","stem":"In a medical journal report, the observed mortality of smokers and non-smokers for lung cancer was reported to be significant at p< 0.05. Such statement means that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The investigator is rejecting the null hypothesis even though the results could occur purely by chance a maximum of 5 times out of 100"},{"label":"B","text":"There is a difference between mortality rates of smokers and non smokers 5% of the time"},{"label":"C","text":"The null hypothesis claims that there is a difference between the mortality rates of smokers and non-smokers"},{"label":"D","text":"A causal relationship between smoking and mortality may be established through the study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-70c6a1712729","stem":"What is the claim of the null hypothesis (Ho) in this study in Q 66? What are the following fractions in Q 68 -72?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The null hypothesis claims that there is a difference between the mortality rates of smokers and non-smokers"},{"label":"B","text":"The null hypothesis claims that there is no difference between the mortality rates of smokers and non-smokers"},{"label":"C","text":"The null hypothesis claims that there is a difference between the mortality rates of smokers and non-smokers 5% of time"},{"label":"D","text":"The null hypothesis claims that there is no difference between the mortality rates of smokers and non-smokers 95% of the time"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e78c7bdf426a","stem":"Number of children < 365 days of age who died in district A in 2008 Number of live births in district A in 2008","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion"},{"label":"C","text":"Rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence proportion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-79a6865f0803","stem":"Number of neonatal deaths in district A in 2008 * 1,000 Total number of live births in same year","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion"},{"label":"C","text":"Rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence proportion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3152c5f892a3","stem":"Number of maternal deaths in district A in 2008 * 100,000 Number of live births in district A in 2008","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion"},{"label":"C","text":"Rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence proportion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-780b57fb8b9f","stem":"Number of all deaths in district A in 2008 * 1,000 Total midyear population","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion"},{"label":"C","text":"Rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence proportion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0cd7a4f7fa93","stem":"Number of deaths due to specific disease in district A in 2008 * 1,000 Total midyear population in district A in 2008","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion"},{"label":"C","text":"Rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence proportion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-64185557309b","stem":"Disease X is incurable. It is known that the incidence of Disease X has been constant during the past 30 years. Researchers have determined that the prevalence of Disease X is higher today than it was 15 years ago. Select the answer that best explains this observation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients with Disease X lived longer 15 years ago compared to today"},{"label":"B","text":"The attributable risk of Disease X has increased during the past 15 years"},{"label":"C","text":"The odds ratio of getting Disease X has increased during the past 15 years"},{"label":"D","text":"Patients with Disease X had a shorter life span 15 years ago compared to today"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"476a86fadea40a032b88b7afac7c31336cb1ec74"},{"id":"q-c4db444684fa","stem":"You are the DMO of district X. The reporter approached you to comment on the trend of Tuberculosis which have been recorded in the district from 1994 to present. What will be your comment? [pic]","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"You cannot comment on trend because population at risk not known"},{"label":"B","text":"You cannot comment on trend using number of cases"},{"label":"C","text":"Incidence of TB is rising from 1994 to 2012"},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence of TB does not show much change over the years"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-363e8b7f4206","stem":"A descriptive cross-sectional study was conducted to measure HIV prevalence among pregnant women (aged 15-35 years) attending antenatal clinics in Kampala, Uganda. The exposure in this study is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"being pregnant"},{"label":"B","text":"being aged 15-35"},{"label":"C","text":"a & b are correct"},{"label":"D","text":"there is no exposure"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a342c536f785","stem":"At an initial examination in Kasulu migraine headache was found in 5 of 1,000 men aged 30 to 35 years and in 10 of 1,000 women aged 30 to 35 years. The inference that women have a two times greater risk of developing migraine headache than do men in this age group is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Correct"},{"label":"B","text":"Incorrect, because a ratio has been used to compare male and female rates"},{"label":"C","text":"Incorrect, because of failure to distinguish between incidence and prevalence"},{"label":"D","text":"Incorrect, because of failure to recognize the cohort effect of age in the two groups"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e7a407039ac7","stem":"A survey was conducted among 15-45 year-olds in a population to investigate whether gender is associated with cigarette smoking. The prevalence of cigarette smoking was 28% for males aged 15-45 years and 4% for females aged 15-45 years. In this study","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cigarette smoking is the outcome and gender is the exposure"},{"label":"B","text":"there is no outcome: gender and smoking are both exposures"},{"label":"C","text":"cigarette smoking is the outcome and age 15-45 years is the exposure"},{"label":"D","text":"the results suggest the effect ofage on smoking is modified by gender"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1772e47038a2","stem":"A cohort study is undertaken to assess the impact of obesity on risk of heart disease. At baseline it is important to exclude the following people","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"People who are not obese"},{"label":"B","text":"People who are obese"},{"label":"C","text":"People with prevalent heart disease"},{"label":"D","text":"a and c"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-60663ddf9236","stem":"In the same study above (Q 78), the dependent variable is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obesity"},{"label":"B","text":"None-obese"},{"label":"C","text":"Heart disease"},{"label":"D","text":"No heart disease"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2b16526ad128","stem":"In the same study as in Q 78 above, 1000 people were recruited. During 5 years of follow-up, 100 were lost to follow-up and 90 developed heart disease. Which of the following statements is correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The risk of heart disease was 0.09 or 9%"},{"label":"B","text":"The risk of heart disease was 0.10 or 10%"},{"label":"C","text":"The risk cannot be estimated because of loss to follow-up"},{"label":"D","text":"The rate of heart disease was 2 per 100 person-years"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-20b829dde62d","stem":"The incidence rate of a disease is five times greater in women than men, but the prevalence show no sex difference. The best explanation is that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The crude all- cause mortality rate is greater in women"},{"label":"B","text":"The case fatality rate for this disease is greater for women"},{"label":"C","text":"The case fatality rate for this disease is lower in women"},{"label":"D","text":"The duration of this disease is shorter in men"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30a31096df9b","stem":"Regarding incidence rate which is false?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a measure of risk of developing disease over specified period"},{"label":"B","text":"Is estimated from dynamic cohort or open cohort"},{"label":"C","text":"Measure force of morbidity at instant of time"},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as incidence density"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6adc676fec7a","stem":"A survey of deafness was conducted in a district in Mali. A representative sample of 3000 people was selected from the population. These people were examined for deafness and 60 cases were identified. The total population of the district is 1,000,000. How many people with deafness are there expected to be in the district?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cannot estimate using the available data"},{"label":"B","text":"2,000"},{"label":"C","text":"20,000"},{"label":"D","text":"200,000"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2ec1dabe83d4","stem":"Regarding cumulative incidence, which is false?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a measure of effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Can vary from 0 to 100%"},{"label":"C","text":"Estimates risk of developing an outcome among all members at risk in a specified period"},{"label":"D","text":"Assume follow up period is the same for population at risk"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6fe05d093331","stem":"Blood types can be A, B, AB and O. An individual cannot have two blood types. A researcher determines the frequency of these blood types in a group of 150 medical students. Select the best statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The researcher might need to perform a transformation on the data results."},{"label":"B","text":"Blood types are an example of nominal data."},{"label":"C","text":"It would be appropriate to perform ANOVA if the researcher wanted to assess the proportion of blood types by sex."},{"label":"D","text":"Blood types are an example of ordinal data."},{"label":"E","text":"The standard error of the mean can be calculated for these frequencies according to the formula of the standard deviation divided by the square root of n."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e71b4340c8b2","stem":"In an Asian country with a population of 6 million people, 60,000 deaths occurred during the year ending Dec 31st, 2001. These included 30,000 deaths of cholera in 100,000 people who were sick with cholera. The case-fatality rate from cholera in 2001 was","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5%"},{"label":"B","text":"50%...... CRUDE RATE"},{"label":"C","text":"1%"},{"label":"D","text":"30%"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-43c6548258fd","stem":"If we wanted to obtain a measure of the incidence of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro during 2012, what breast cancer cases would we count in the numerator?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2012"},{"label":"B","text":"Only newly diagnosed cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2012"},{"label":"C","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro region"},{"label":"D","text":"All women at reproductive age only at Kilimanjaro SECTION 2: Short answer question (10 marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a6ef3c6ffb7","stem":"If we took the 500 people attending a school in New York City, divided them by gender, and then took a random sample of the males and a random sampling of the females, the variable on which we would divide the population is called the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dependent variable b) Stratification variable"},{"label":"C","text":"Sampling variable"},{"label":"D","text":"Frame sampling"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c2e36e409c71e4f34fe4e42e9ceb7ed68099181d"},{"id":"q-aa9b3480fe9b","stem":"______ research occurs when the researcher manipulates the independent variable.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"causal-comparative research"},{"label":"B","text":"experimental research c. ethnography"},{"label":"C","text":"correlational research"},{"label":"D","text":"Cross sectional research"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"158d76426582f498533c42e96a06ce1a0d6c43e7"},{"id":"q-21fb6fcba92f","stem":"A good qualitative problem statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defines the independent and dependent variables"},{"label":"B","text":"Conveys a sense of emerging design"},{"label":"C","text":"Specifies a research hypothesis to be tested"},{"label":"D","text":"Specifies the relationship between variables that the researcher expects to find"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2aa0f1edd23d","stem":"The statement of purpose in a research study should","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Identify the design of the study"},{"label":"B","text":"Identify the intent or objective of the study"},{"label":"C","text":"Specify the type of people to be used in the study"},{"label":"D","text":"Describe the study"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3e2f0785eff2","stem":"A qualitative research question","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asks a question about some process, or phenomenon to be explored"},{"label":"B","text":"Is generally an open-ended question"},{"label":"C","text":"both a and b are correct"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f842f156867f","stem":"Is the following qualitative research purpose statement “well stated” or “poorly stated”? “The focus of the present study was to explore distressing and nurturing encounters of patients with caregivers and to ascertain the meanings that are engendered by such encounters. The study was conducted on one of the surgical units and the obstetrical/gynecological unit of a 374-bed community hospital.”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a well stated"},{"label":"B","text":"It is poorly stated"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"693a9af545c5ebb7d4d85de1b1fa16ca7f33b89c"},{"id":"q-1dfc4b39358b","stem":"2143 what can you say about the probability of a complications in the entire population of patients who will be treated with this drug? Two things must have been occurred here","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is the 14 people representative of the entire population................"},{"label":"B","text":"The issue of random sampling........................."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e0d29f5c24573a2e19dfbfb938cb2c47fd89df7d"},{"id":"q-47f41ccfe736","stem":"Randomization is aiming at making the result of the study generalisable......t........ SECTION III: SHORTS NOTES QUESTIONS Q: 1 Read the scenario and answer questions that follow (15 marks) BMI is a measure of obesity (weight / height2). A study set out compare BMI between men and women. Mean BMI in a sample of men was 26.8 and mean BMI in a sample of women was 29.0. The difference between the mean for women and the mean for men was 2.2, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.1 to 4.3. The P value for this difference was calculated to be 0.048 (i.e. P = 0.048). 2 a) Based on the information of this study, suggest the title for this table? 2 b) i: Was this study descriptive or analytical? prevalence of parasitaemia?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What would the null hypothesis be?"},{"label":"B","text":"What would the study hypothesis be?"},{"label":"C","text":"What was the objective of this study?"},{"label":"D","text":"Name the measure central tendency used in this study ................................................................................................"},{"label":"E","text":"Explain why do you think this measure of central tendency was used instead of others"},{"label":"F","text":"What would you comment on the difference observed, in terms of Magnitude................................................................................. Direction.................................................................................... Statistical.................................................................................."},{"label":"G","text":"Name independent................................................................and dependent..................................................................................... Variables from this study Q: 2 Read the scenario and respond to questions below (15 marks) In 1997 investigator carried out a cross sectional study of children 2 years of age in 18 villages in Tanzania at the beginning of a marketing campaign to promote the use of treated nets. They collected data on net ownership and other factors by questionnaire (the mothers of children were interviewed), and took blood samples from the children in the study to assess whether they were infected with malaria parasites (parasitaemia), and also to see whether they were anaemic (as a consequence). The children were also assessed for clinical signs of malaria. Two further surveys were done over the next 2 years. A different random sample of children was selected on each occasion. The investigator identified 985 eligible children, and interviewed the mothers of 827 of these children over the course of three cross sectional surveys (16 mother refused consent, and 142 could not be contacted). Of the 827 interviewees, data from 748 were included in the analysis because 68 children were aged over 2 years at the time of sampling and net status was not known for 11 children. Table 1 below show some of the results from this study Table: 1 | |Year of survey | | |1997 |1998 |1999 | |No of children eligible |325 |330 |330 | |No of children analyzed |240 |269 |239 | |No (%) ownership | | | | |No net |100 (42%) |49 (18%) |40 (17%) | |Untreated net |116 (48%) |64 (24%) |53 (22%) | |Treated net |24 (10%) |156 (58%) |146 (61%) | |No (%) of children | | | | |With anemia |118 (49%) |83 (31%) |62 (26%) | |With parasitaemia |151 (63%) |126 (47%) |90 (38%) | |With splenomegally |207 (86%) |144 (54%) |117 (49%) | Source: data from Abdullah et al (2001) Questions: ii: Name the study design used in this survey .............................................................................................. 2 c) Does it matter that data from some of the eligible children were not included in the analysis?........................................................................ 2 d) Describe the results of the study shown in the table above focus on the number of net ownership and number of children with parasitaemia across years........................................................................................ .............................. 2 e) Restrict yourself to year 1997 and 1999; what is the prevalence ratio for the effect of owning a treated net on the .............................. Q: 3 A study was conducted by a police traffic officer from the existing records in the police station from his report he first stated that 10% of all road accidents are due to drunken driving, and before allowing question from the audience he over emphasized the seriousness of the accidents and lastly concluded that it is logical to state that 90% of all remaining road accidents are due to driving without drinking (10 marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"9fb569dced41e03bf0b49c77573d64a62f92363e"},{"id":"q-5c90e7c88e41","stem":"a) What kind of study design does the figure above represent?","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3ca945f3e0d5","stem":"Which of the following is NOT correct about the principles of causal inference?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lack of Temporal Ambiguity means that the cause should precede the effect"},{"label":"B","text":"A Dose-Response Relationship means that the size of an effect should vary with the size of the cause."},{"label":"C","text":"Coherence means that there is biological or theoretical basis for the hypothesized cause and effect."},{"label":"D","text":"Specificity means that the effects are associated primarily with one cause"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b66d916a63879d393af3003dbf0188ce24570be1"},{"id":"q-8a65b171cb36","stem":"Which one of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pie charts are better than bar graphs for comparing relative sizes."},{"label":"B","text":"Data that are nominal scale are presented using frequency tables."},{"label":"C","text":"Means and standard deviation of ordinal data are meaningless."},{"label":"D","text":"The scatter-plot is the basic graphic tool for investigating relationships between two interval or ratio scaled variables."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9b57d62be4e747c314e4219d8143815571529b28"},{"id":"q-a2d6e39c3692","stem":"In measuring the centre of the data from a skewed distribution, the median would be preferred over the mean for most purposes because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the median is the most frequent number while the mean is most likely"},{"label":"B","text":"the mean may be too heavily influenced by the larger observations and this gives too high an indication of the centre"},{"label":"C","text":"the median is less than the mean and smaller numbers are always appropriate for the centre"},{"label":"D","text":"the mean measures the spread in the data"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0212f855ce0","stem":"Which of the following statements is NOT true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In a symmetric distribution, the mean and the median are equal."},{"label":"B","text":"The first quartile is equal to the twenty-fifth percentile."},{"label":"C","text":"In a symmetric distribution, the median is halfway between the first and the third quartiles."},{"label":"D","text":"The median is always greater than the mean."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7ce3019b6ded553e3d04663fba4a7c455dafac68"},{"id":"q-b9365407d3e2","stem":"We wish to investigate if a new medicine is effective in reducing the length and severity of the flu. We take the next 20 patients that come to the walk-in clinic complaining of flu and, after a medical exam to verify that the patients do have the flu, we give them the new medicine and tell them about the new drug we are giving them. One week later, the patients are contacted and 15 patients state the new remedy was helpful in reducing the severity and length of the illness. Which of the following is NOT CORRECT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This is a poor experiment because there is no control group. We do not know how many would feel better in a week without treatment."},{"label":"B","text":"This is a poor experiment because it is not double-blinded. The patients may feel relief because they thought the drug should work."},{"label":"C","text":"This is a poor experiment because a convenience sample was selected."},{"label":"D","text":"This is a poor experiment because we didn’t give the remedy to people without the flu to assess its effect in a control group."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8d6a1c5dfcc0a6e4ab553f9ab636b840bf44561f"},{"id":"q-7971348911a4","stem":"A survey is to be undertaken of recent nursing graduates in order to compare the starting salaries of women and men. For each graduate, three variables are to be recorded (among others) u sex, starting salary, and area of specialization.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sex and starting salary are explanatory variables; area of specialization is a response variable."},{"label":"B","text":"Sex is an explanatory variable; starting salary and area of specialization are response variables."},{"label":"C","text":"Sex is an explanatory variable; starting salary is a response variable; area of specialization is a possible confounding variable."},{"label":"D","text":"Sex is a response variable; starting salary is an explanatory variable; area of specialization is a possible confounding variable."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-745304d24a5d","stem":"Which of the following is CORRECT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"We do not need to randomize if our sample size is sufficiently large."},{"label":"B","text":"A large sample size always ensures that our sample is representative of the population."},{"label":"C","text":"If all other things are equal, we need a larger sample size for a larger population."},{"label":"D","text":"In a properly chosen sample, an estimate will be less variable with a large sample size and hence more precise."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dd94cde0d572d59d908db1abc4ef5afbb72d56d7"},{"id":"q-938559e7d397","stem":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"No response can cause bias in surveys because non-respondents often tend to behave differently from people who respond."},{"label":"B","text":"Non-sampling errors are often bigger than the random sampling errors in surveys."},{"label":"C","text":"People will sometimes answer a question differently for different interviewers."},{"label":"D","text":"Sophisticated statistical methods can always correct the results if the"},{"label":"E","text":"Population you are sampling from is different from the population of interest, e.g. due to under-coverage."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddd53843a7c3208057acb162a2c5585b42e5b16c"},{"id":"q-ce760c05a15f","stem":"A condition or characteristic that can take on different values or categories is called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a constant"},{"label":"B","text":"a variable"},{"label":"C","text":"a cause-and-effect relationship"},{"label":"D","text":"a descriptive relationship"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dae28f5ff2eb","stem":"The group that receives the experimental treatment condition is the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimental group"},{"label":"B","text":"Control group"},{"label":"C","text":"Participant group"},{"label":"D","text":"Independent group"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-45fb8010cc9c","stem":"In an experimental research study, the primary goal is to isolate and identify the effect produced by the ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dependent variable"},{"label":"B","text":"Extraneous variable"},{"label":"C","text":"Independent variable"},{"label":"D","text":"Confounding variable"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce8fbcdf042f","stem":"Which method of controlling confounding extraneous variables takes precedence over all other methods?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Matching individual participants"},{"label":"B","text":"Building the extraneous variable into the research design"},{"label":"C","text":"Counterbalancing"},{"label":"D","text":"Randomly assign research participants to the groups"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ae6e868fdcf6","stem":"Which of the following is not an example of a nonrandom sampling technique?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purposive"},{"label":"B","text":"Quota"},{"label":"C","text":"Convenience"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9bda8cb8029c5d4855f94c4fc157c7465dc9f4ee"},{"id":"q-e5f23882bdbb","stem":"What options do you have to summarize not normally distributed data?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Give the population mean, and median"},{"label":"B","text":"Give the geometric mean"},{"label":"C","text":"Answers b and a are correct"},{"label":"D","text":"Answers a, b and c are correct True/False Questions ( 25 Questions, total 25 Marks) Indicate the letter T for True statement and letter F for a false statement"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ad9ba40f67b05a81c3790213226fc3d2f253c56e"},{"id":"q-362926171c32","stem":"When there is no ordering (eg ethnic group), this is sometimes called an unordered or nominal categorical variable (T)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-239b74e0af38","stem":"Name the type of variable indicated in this table..............................","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f114a05181e6","stem":"What is the total percentage of ex-smokers?.......................................","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-110702d0def6","stem":"What will be the proportion of the never smokers?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89bf8054898a","stem":"In January there were 50 cases of malaria diagnosed in District X (population 200 000). Of these cases, 30 were males and 20 females. Question: What proportion of the cases was in males?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ab97b93f5d6","stem":"An exposure List B","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This means that the disease is not associated with the exposure in the population under study: the finding is simply wrong."},{"label":"B","text":"This means that although the disease is associated with the exposure, some other factor (which is associated with the exposure and independently associated with the disease) can explain this association."},{"label":"C","text":"This is especially a problem in case-control studies, when cases may recall their exposure more completely than controls"},{"label":"D","text":"Causes and incidence of disease"},{"label":"E","text":"Disease description"},{"label":"F","text":"Longitudinal and follow-ups"},{"label":"G","text":"Random selection"},{"label":"H","text":"Random sample I) Not statistically significant J) Is statistically significant K) Sampling variation L) Sampling distribution M) Standard error N) Measures amount of variability in the population O) Measure amount of variability in the sample mean"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-44f47a494423","stem":"List the key words that must be in the definition of epidemiology","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-04c8cdcb6e32","stem":"List any five Bradford – Hill criteria for causation","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81437349e208","stem":"List two characteristics features of a confounding variable that must have to have a confounding effect","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-68e1639367c6","stem":"Which of the following statements about confidence intervals is INCORRECT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"If we keep the sample size fixed, the confidence interval gets wider as we increase the confidence coefficient."},{"label":"B","text":"A confidence interval for a mean always contains the sample mean."},{"label":"C","text":"If we keep the confidence coefficient fixed, the confidence interval gets narrower as we increase the sample size."},{"label":"D","text":"If the population standard deviation increases, the confidence interval decreases in width."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27a5de9ab732","stem":"You have measured the systolic blood pressure of a random sample of 25 employees of a company. A 95% confidence interval for the mean systolic blood pressure for the employees is computed to be (122,138). Which of the following statements gives a valid interpretation of this interval?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"About 95% of the sample of employees have a systolic blood pressure between 122 and 138."},{"label":"B","text":"About 95% of the employees in the company have a systolic blood pressure between 122 and 138."},{"label":"C","text":"If the sampling procedure were repeated many times, then approximately 95% of the resulting confidence intervals would contain the mean systolic blood pressure for employees in the company."},{"label":"D","text":"If the sampling procedure were repeated many times, then approximately 95% of the sample means would be between 122 and 138."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a8c8b6dcd28f15a35b28406c4b1dd9090047c07d"},{"id":"q-529c675346fc","stem":"Systematic error is associated with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reliability"},{"label":"B","text":"Validity"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigur